The 4 October export contradicts the [exported incident summary](/kb/incidents/4471/):
one merged ticket contains two classified causes. The fixes need separate tracking.
The evidence below describes that historical snapshot. A later engineering update supplies
the closure outcomes; it does not add new report data or prove each unusual symptom resolved.

## Engineering outcome · 5 October 2026

The [course engineering update](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows#engineering-update)
reports that #4471 was closed on 5 October. It gives different outcomes for the two causes:

- **Parser — fixed:** engineering reports `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
  [GitHub issue #1](https://github.com/raphoq/ai-course/issues/1) is closed as completed
  on the strength of that report, not a production test performed by this review.
- **Form hint — won't fix:** booking-v3 was retired.
  [GitHub issue #2](https://github.com/raphoq/ai-course/issues/2) is closed as not planned;
  this is retirement of the affected form, not a claim that its hint was corrected.

The source update was read on 28 October 2026; its screenshot is included in repository
evidence. The counts remain those of the 4 October export. Decision 0002 remains proposed.

## Evidence and counts

<IncidentReview />

## Separate the causes · historical diagnosis

**Parser:** the shared parser reads month first for day-first tenants. A date such as
7 April becomes 4 July. Keep the parser reports together across app, email and the
booking-v2 form. Reports from booking-v3 tenants through app or email still belong here.
The required fix was parsing according to the tenant's date contract and verifying invite round trips.
Track the five unusual bodies above as unresolved evidence within this ticket.

**Form hint:** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` while the form reads dates in the tenant's
day-first order. Users follow the displayed hint and submit a date the form interprets
differently. This affects form reports for Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour.
The proposed remediation was to align the hint and form validation with the tenant's locale.
A parser fix alone would not correct that instruction. Engineering instead retired booking-v3.

The exact report IDs and reproducible count output are in `evidence/analysis.json`.
See the repository README for publication status and both GitHub issue links.

## Why the queue stayed wrong

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/) matches subjects alone.
That can hide distinct causes behind identical wording, and can split one cause across
different subjects. The live examples below compute this from tenant locale, form version
and channel. Unknown tenants require investigation before merging.

<DedupeExplorer />

The original explorer above illustrates the exported classifier. Its “same cause — merge”
label is a preliminary assignment, not proof of a shared cause or permission to merge.
Read the bodies and resolve conflicting evidence before deciding.

Review proposed [decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause/) for future triage;
adopt it only after the support desk accepts it. The accepted
record 0001 remains intact as history. The old triage page was last reviewed in 2024;
its accepted status does not make it current. Check rendered components and source data
before relying on prose, even when an incident page was reviewed recently.

## Timing and follow-up

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Compare arrival time with merge time before treating a queue spike as a new outage.
During an active incident, reconcile new reports by ID, reclassify against the tenant
directory and retain uncertain symptoms. This export is not a live queue.
For the one-day and cross-city reports, request the original date input, timezone,
stored timestamp, invite payload and affected form version. These reports suggest further
investigation; this export does not prove a timezone defect or any specific third cause.

The engineering update above is the authority for closing the two tracking issues; a
queued fix alone would not have justified closure. The five unusual bodies remain unresolved
evidence in this snapshot. Reopen or split follow-up work if a reproduction establishes a
remaining defect. Have the support desk review the replacement decision before adopting it.

